"Google Open-Sources Harness2, a Recursive Agent Harnessing Framework Google Cloud AI Research has unveiled Harness², an open-source framework that improves agent performance by recursively optimizing surrounding environmental elements such as prompts and tools without altering model weights. While keeping model weights fixed, Harness² manages eight harness components—including system prompts, guardrails, memory, and skills—as files, recursively modifying and optimizing them. To function even in open-world environments without ground-truth labels or verifiers, it introduces a two-stage recursive architecture consisting of Propose, Probe, and Compose. With just a single refinement step (k=1), it demonstrated performance improvements across all three tested benchmarks and four model-harness configurations. How to Make Zero-Training Tabular Predictions with NVIDIA Structured Data Models Introduces how to use NVIDIA's Structured Data Models library, which enables predictions on tabular data and relational databases via in-context learning without requiring separate additional training. NVIDIA's Structured Data Models provides pretrained foundation models for tabular and relational data—such as TabICLv2, KumoTabular, and KumoRelational—integrated into a unified PyTorch interface. Using an in-context learning approach that passes labeled example rows and target prediction rows to the model, probability values or predictive distributions can be obtained in a single forward pass. Covers caveats when using default configurations—such as default preprocessing omitting timestamp and text columns or pitfalls in explanation tools—as well as tips for handling relational data. Meet Voxel51 3.0: A Unified Multimodal Data Platform for Physical AI Voxel51 has launched Voxel51 3.0, a unified multimodal data platform supporting search, curation, annotation, and evaluation for Physical AI data, including robots, autonomous vehicles, and factory sensors. Added three core features: time-series multimodal support, unified annotation, and vision-language model-based agentic labeling Applied a LanceDB-based hybrid search index to simultaneously support semantic search alongside lexical and structured queries, while breaking down data provenance tracking into four granular tiers Equipped with Voxel51 Agent supporting over 45 MCP tools and more than 80 operators, offering a free self-service tier Show GN: AIPath - A Service for Testing Generative AI Knowledge Through Role-Based Exams and Explanations An introduction to AIPath, an assessment service that allows users to evaluate and learn knowledge and situational judgment skills across major generative AI services through role-specific and certification exams. Comprehensively covers various generative AI ecosystems and service differences across OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google, alongside development and security knowledge. Consists of 10 certification areas ranging from foundational use to workflow automation, application development, security, and governance, backed by a question bank of 855 items. Conducts evaluations grounded in official documentation, with results available for image export or LinkedIn link sharing. Show GN: SIGLENS: An Analysis Service Where LLMs Interpret Market Data Calculated by Code An introduction to SIGLENS, an open-source investment analysis service where LLMs interpret context and generate reports based on highly reliable financial indicators calculated by code. To prevent LLM hallucination, indicator calculation and signal determination are handled by code, while the LLM's role is separated to interpreting and explaining the calculation results. Provides chart indicators, options, and ownership disclosure data for US and Korean stocks as well as cryptocurrencies, supporting ATR-based price validation and email report features. Built on Next.js, it open-sources the application code (MIT) and calculation logic, offering a free tier and supporting a BYOK (Bring Your Own Key) model. Show HN: Sphinx - A Git hook that tests your understanding of AI-generated code before deployment An introduction to Sphinx, a Git hook tool that verifies whether developers properly understand how AI-generated code behaves and handles edge cases before deploying or pushing it, by quizzing them on the code. When pushing to a protected branch, it pauses Git and presents a quiz based on the diff about code behavior such as failure cases, requiring correct answers before the push can proceed. It supports various coding agents including Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, and OpenCode, as well as OpenRouter/OpenAI-compatible endpoints; in emergencies, it can be bypassed using the sphinx yolo command, though the history is logged. It requires Node 22.19 or higher (macOS, Linux, WSL) and is distributed as free open source under the package name sphinx-understand. DeepSeek Search and Page Fetch APIs Priced at $0.75 per 1,000 Successful Requests DeepSeek has launched Search and Fetch APIs that support real-time web search and page Markdown body extraction for AI agents and RAG applications. Both Search API and Fetch API are priced at $0.75 per 1,000 successful requests, billed only on HTTP 200 responses that return actual results. Powered by its proprietary search engine, Search API supports site, language, and date filters, providing structured summaries for UIs and models along with relevance scores. Server-side tool calls originating from DeepSeek Harness, Claude Code, Codex, and others are currently free and will become paid starting November 1, 2026. Issue with Codex Desktop Continuously Generating Crashpad Pending Dumps An issue where the Codex Desktop app on macOS exhausts disk space due to an abnormally large number of pending dump files generated by a crash loop within the Crashpad error handler itself While the Codex Desktop app runs normally, browser_crashpad_handler repeatedly crashes and restarts in the background, accumulating dump files at a rate of roughly one per second A Mach IPC error in the handler (mach_port_request_notification failure) combined with self-monitoring functionality causes continuous dump file generation, amounting to approximately 0.6 GB per hour and up to 15 GB per day Deleting files or reinstalling the app does not resolve the issue, and users are demanding fundamental fixes such as storage limits and rotation/cleanup policies for dump files ICLR 2027: Reciprocal reviewer with 0 paper assignments—should I be concerned? [D] An inquiry from a researcher who registered as a reciprocal reviewer for the ICLR 2027 conference but is concerned about not receiving any paper assignments. The author mentions participating as an ICLR 2027 reciprocal reviewer but has not yet been assigned any papers to review Asks if other reciprocal reviewers are experiencing the same situation regarding paper non-assignment Seeks community feedback and advice on whether this situation is a cause for concern GPT-2 KV cache from scratch: correctness, latency, and memory measurements [P] A project that implements a KV cache for the GPT-2 model from scratch using PyTorch and compares theoretical savings against actual latency and memory measurements. Confirmed that logit discrepancies with the Hugging Face implementation matched closely at around 6.1e-5, and verified cache concatenation ordering during single-token and multi-token decoding. At a context length of 1,000, FLOP computation decreased by about 1,000x, but actual per-token latency only improved by about 6x from 29.69 ms to 4.95 ms (and by about 2.9x from 14.66 seconds to 5.03 seconds when generating 1,016 tokens). Identified and accounted for an 8,519,688-byte discrepancy between theoretical memory (72 KiB per token) and actual measurements, showing it was due to the cuBLAS workspace and the next-token tensor. [P] asmsense: 228k stripped x86-64 functions with source-aware labels, and a map of the data we still lack. [P] An article introducing the asmsense project—a dataset and model featuring source-aware labels to predict names and descriptions of stripped x86-64 binary functions—and discussing directions for future improvements. Released a dataset of 228,177 functions compiled with -O0 through -Os optimization flags across 341 permissively licensed C projects, along with a Qwen3-8B + LoRA-based model. Current data is limited to clang/Mach-O on macOS, necessitating expansion to other architectures such as ARM64 and RISC-V, GCC/ELF builds, and Chinese descriptions. Soliciting community feedback on designing reward functions and building reinforcement learning (RL) environments that compile, strip, and evaluate function name predictions. OpenAI Upgrades 'GPT-6 Astra Pro'... Supporting Parallel Execution of Agents OpenAI has upgraded GPT-6 Astra Pro, enabling ChatGPT Pro users to run multiple AI agents in parallel and monitor their real-time progress. Added a parallel execution capability allowing a single task to be broken down and processed concurrently and independently by multiple AI agents. Users can directly track the individual progress and review the completed work of each agent on the chat interface. Response speed and the use of tools and plugins have been improved, with initial rollout currently available to select ChatGPT Pro plan users. [New AI Models] 6 New Releases Including Video Generation 'Kling 4.0' and Emotion-Controlled Voice 'Drama 3' Six diverse cutting-edge AI models—including video generation model 'Kling 4.0', voice generation model 'Drama 3', real-time gaming, and on-device models—have been newly unveiled. Kling AI officially launched 'Kling 4.0', which supports 30-second video generation and MCP/CLI integration. Fish Audio's 'Drama 3' is a voice model allowing fine-grained control over emotion and tone, supporting 83 languages with a cost 5 times cheaper than before. Skywork's real-time game video generation model 'Matrix Game 4.0', PrismML's ultra-lightweight on-device model 'Bonsai 2', Kyutai's robot control VLA model 'REKI', and fal's real-time virtual space exploration model 'fal World' were also unveiled. Google Reduces Critical Quantum Computing Error to 1/40th... A Clue to Practical Application Google Quantum AI demonstrated 'magic state cultivation' technology on its proprietary quantum processor 'Willow,' reducing key quantum computing errors to about 1/40th of previous levels. Google Quantum AI implemented 'magic state cultivation' technology on the quantum processor 'Willow' and published the findings in the international academic journal 'Science.' Instead of the conventional distillation method, it improved states step-by-step and discarded erroneous ones, lowering the error rate to about 1/40th and achieving a T-state fidelity of 99.99%. While confirming the potential for connection to actual computation, challenges remain for practical application and large-scale expansion, such as a strict experimental criteria pass rate of only about 8% and the occurrence of additional errors. DeepSeek Releases ‘V4.1 Flash’ Update with Enhanced Coding Performance and Unveils Search API DeepSeek has unveiled its 'V4.1 Flash' model, which improves coding and agent performance while reducing task time and output tokens to increase cost efficiency, along with a new search API. DeepSeek released the 'DeepSeek V4.1-Flash-1017' model, open-sourced under the MIT license, which lowers actual costs by reducing task time and output tokens. Scoring 77.0 on the software development benchmark DeepSWE v1.1, it outperformed competitor models in some evaluations, enhancing end-to-end project development capabilities through self-feedback reinforcement learning. Opened the 'Search' and 'Fetch' APIs, supporting web search and webpage data retrieval, at a rate of $0.75 per 1,000 successful requests."